## 5.4. Charges
The evolution of charge on Ni during catalysis were also explored and are summarized in Table 2. Again, we found an excellent agreement between QM/MM and full QM results, with the maximum deviation being 0.019 (intermediate 2), six of the eight deviations being 0.009 or less, and the mean unsigned deviation being only 0.006. Accordingly, the fluctuation of the charge on Ni during catalysis from full QM calculations is well reproduced by QM/MM calculations (see Figure 7).
## 5.5. Efficiency
A key motivation for using QM/MM methods is computational efficiency. For optimizing the equilibrium points (i.e., 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5) involved in the catalytic cycle using 24 CPU cores, the mean cost for full QM was 160 hours and 31 minutes, and that for QM/MM was 5 hours 27 minutes (see Table S17  for details), giving rise to a savings of a factor of 29 for QM/MM with respect to full QM. Optimizing saddle points was much more costly than optimizing equilibrium points, and again there is a huge savings, although the way that the calculations were done did not afford the opportunity for a direct comparison of costs (see Discussion S2).
